Now the error changed from 502 → 400 Bad Request, which is actually good 👍.
This means:

✅ Nginx is working
✅ Gunicorn is working
✅ Django is receiving the request

But Django is rejecting the request because of ALLOWED_HOSTS.

Fix: Update ALLOWED_HOSTS

Open Django settings:

nano crm/settings.py

Find this line:

ALLOWED_HOSTS = []

Change it to your EC2 public IP:

ALLOWED_HOSTS = ['YOUR_PUBLIC_IP']

Example:

ALLOWED_HOSTS = ['13.233.120.45']

For testing you can also temporarily use:

ALLOWED_HOSTS = ['*']

⚠️ '*' is fine for testing but not recommended for production.

Restart Gunicorn

After saving the file run:

sudo systemctl restart gunicorn
Then Open in Browser
http://YOUR_PUBLIC_IP

Your Django CRM site should load 🎉

Why This Happens

When DEBUG = False, Django only allows requests from domains listed in ALLOWED_HOSTS.

So when you access using the EC2 public IP, Django blocks it and returns 400 Bad Request.

