502 Bad Gateway
nginx/1.24.0 (Ubuntu)

A 502 Bad Gateway from Nginx means Nginx cannot reach Gunicorn (or Gunicorn crashed). Since it worked earlier and now shows 502, we need to check three things: Gunicorn status, socket file, and Nginx config.

Follow these steps in order 👇

1️⃣ Check Gunicorn Status

Run:

sudo systemctl status gunicorn

If it shows inactive or failed, restart it:

sudo systemctl restart gunicorn
2️⃣ Check Gunicorn Logs
journalctl -u gunicorn -n 50 --no-pager

Look for errors like:

ModuleNotFoundError

ImportError

Permission denied

Application failed to start

3️⃣ Check if Socket File Exists

Your Nginx connects to Gunicorn using a socket.

Run:

ls /root/My_CRM/

Look for:

gunicorn.sock

If gunicorn.sock is missing, Gunicorn did not start properly.

4️⃣ Check Nginx Error Log (Most Important)
sudo tail -50 /var/log/nginx/error.log

This will show the exact reason for 502.

Typical message:

connect() to unix:/root/My_CRM/gunicorn.sock failed
5️⃣ Check Nginx Configuration

Open config:

sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/mycrm

Make sure it matches your Gunicorn socket:

server {
    listen 80;
    server_name YOUR_PUBLIC_IP;

    location / {
        include proxy_params;
        proxy_pass http://unix:/root/My_CRM/gunicorn.sock;
    }

    location /static/ {
        root /root/My_CRM;
    }
}

Then test:

sudo nginx -t

Restart:

sudo systemctl restart nginx
⚠️ Most Likely Problem

Your project is located in:

/root/My_CRM

But Nginx runs as www-data user, which cannot access /root directory.

Quick Fix

Run:

sudo chmod 755 /root
sudo chmod -R 755 /root/My_CRM

Then restart services:

sudo systemctl restart gunicorn
sudo systemctl restart nginx
🧠 Professional Practice (Recommended)

Production apps are usually stored in:

/var/www/

Example:

/var/www/mycrm

This avoids permission issues.

✅ To solve it quickly, send me the output of these two commands:

sudo systemctl status gunicorn

and

sudo tail -20 /var/log/nginx/error.log

I can identify the exact issue in seconds. 🚀

t: 49.36.45.254, server: 43.205.136.192, request: "GET / HTTP/1.1", upstream: "http://unix:/root/My_CRM/gunicorn.sock:/", host: "43.205.136.192"
2026/03/13 09:58:59 [crit] 4150#4150: *8 connect() to unix:/root/My_CRM/gunicorn.sock failed (13: Permission denied) while connecting to upstream, client: 49.36.45.254, server: 43.205.136.192, request: "GET / HTTP/1.1", upstream: "http://unix:/root/My_CRM/gunicorn.sock:/", host: "43.205.136.192"
2026/03/13 09:59:11 [crit] 4150#4150: *8 connect() to unix:/root/My_CRM/gunicorn.sock failed (13: Permission denied) while connecting to upstream, client: 49.36.45.254, server: 43.205.136.192, request: "GET / HTTP/1.1", upstream: "http://unix:/root/My_CRM/gunicorn.sock:/", host: "43.205.136.192"

Your log clearly shows the problem:

connect() to unix:/root/My_CRM/gunicorn.sock failed (13: Permission denied)

This means Nginx cannot access the Gunicorn socket file created by Gunicorn because it is inside the /root directory.

Nginx runs as the www-data user, and it cannot access /root by default.

✅ Quick Fix (Recommended)
1️⃣ Change permissions of /root and project

Run:

sudo chmod 755 /root
sudo chmod -R 755 /root/My_CRM
2️⃣ Restart services
sudo systemctl restart gunicorn
sudo systemctl restart nginx
3️⃣ Test again

Open:

http://43.205.136.192

Your Django CRM application should load.

⭐ Best Practice (Production)

In real production servers, projects are not stored in /root.
They are usually placed in:

/var/www/

Example:

/var/www/mycrm

Then permissions are clean and secure.

Example structure:

/var/www/mycrm
 ├── venv
 ├── crm
 ├── manage.py
 └── gunicorn.sock
🚀 Even Better Fix (Professional)

Modify your gunicorn.service so the socket has proper permissions.

Edit:

sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/gunicorn.service

Add inside [Service]:

UMask=007

Example:

[Service]
User=root
Group=www-data
WorkingDirectory=/root/My_CRM
UMask=007
ExecStart=/root/My_CRM/venv/bin/gunicorn \
          --workers 3 \
          --bind unix:/root/My_CRM/gunicorn.sock \
          crm.wsgi:application

Reload:

sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart gunicorn
