give step by step

I’ll give you a clear step-by-step process to connect your DNS domain to your Django project running with Nginx + Gunicorn + Django.

Assume:
Server IP = 43.205.136.192

Step 1 — Add DNS Record

Go to your DNS provider (like GoDaddy, Namecheap, or Amazon Route 53).

Create A records:

Type	Name	Value
A	@	43.205.136.192
A	www	43.205.136.192

Example:

Type: A
Host: @
Value: 43.205.136.192
TTL: 300

Wait 5–15 minutes for DNS propagation.

Step 2 — Verify Domain Points to Server

Run:

ping yourdomain.com

If it returns your server IP:

43.205.136.192

DNS is working.

Step 3 — Update Django ALLOWED_HOSTS

Open settings:

nano ~/My_CRM/crm/settings.py

Find:

ALLOWED_HOSTS = []

Change to:

ALLOWED_HOSTS = ['yourdomain.com','www.yourdomain.com']

Example:

ALLOWED_HOSTS = ['mycrm.com','www.mycrm.com']

Restart Gunicorn:

sudo systemctl restart gunicorn
Step 4 — Configure Nginx for Domain

Open Nginx config:

sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/mycrm

Change to:

server {
    listen 80;
    server_name yourdomain.com www.yourdomain.com;

    location / {
        include proxy_params;
        proxy_pass http://unix:/root/My_CRM/gunicorn.sock;
    }

    location /static/ {
        root /root/My_CRM;
    }
}
Step 5 — Enable Config
sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/mycrm /etc/nginx/sites-enabled
Step 6 — Test Nginx
sudo nginx -t

You should see:

syntax is ok
test is successful
Step 7 — Restart Nginx
sudo systemctl restart nginx
Step 8 — Open Website

Open browser:

http://yourdomain.com

Your Django project should load 🎉

Step 9 — Enable HTTPS (Recommended)

Install Certbot.

sudo apt install certbot python3-certbot-nginx

Run:

sudo certbot --nginx

Select your domain.

Now your website will run on:

https://yourdomain.com
