Saving debug log to /var/log/letsencrypt/letsencrypt.log
Enter email address (used for urgent renewal and security notices)
 (Enter 'c' to cancel): c
An e-mail address or --register-unsafely-without-email must be provided.
Ask for help or search for solutions at https://community.letsencrypt.org. See the logfile /var/log/letsencrypt/letsencrypt.log or re-run Certbot with -v for more details.

You ran Certbot with Nginx, but you pressed c, which cancels the process. Certbot needs an email address to register the SSL certificate from Let's Encrypt.

Let’s do it correctly step-by-step.

Step 1 — Run Certbot Again
sudo certbot --nginx
Step 2 — Enter Email

It will ask:

Enter email address (used for urgent renewal and security notices)

Example:

admin@yourdomain.com

Press Enter.

Step 3 — Accept Terms

You will see:

Please read the Terms of Service

Type:

A

and press Enter.

Step 4 — Email Sharing Option

It will ask:

Would you like to share your email with the EFF?

You can type:

N

or

Y

Both are fine.

Step 5 — Select Your Domain

Certbot will show domains from your Amazon Route 53 DNS and Nginx config.

Example:

1: yourdomain.com
2: www.yourdomain.com

Select:

1,2

Press Enter.

Step 6 — Redirect HTTP → HTTPS

It will ask:

1: No redirect
2: Redirect HTTP to HTTPS

Choose:

2

This forces secure HTTPS.

Step 7 — SSL Installed 🎉

You should see:

Congratulations! Your certificate and chain have been saved.

Your site will now work on:

https://yourdomain.com
Step 8 — Test SSL

Open browser:

https://yourdomain.com

You should see a secure lock icon 🔒.

Step 9 — Auto Renewal Check

Check renewal:

sudo certbot renew --dry-run

Certificates from Let's Encrypt renew automatically every 90 days.

✅ Now your Django app running with Gunicorn + Nginx is fully secured with HTTPS.

